  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES water vapor imagery reveals a sharp trough digging \nacross eastern Montana that is supporting a ~998 mb surface low \nalong the Canada/MT/ND border. A strong 45-60 kt 850 mb jet \nstretches from northern MO all the way to southern Manitoba, \ntransporting warm moisture air northward and isentropically forcing \nan area of showers and thunderstorms from Iowa through Minnesota. \nCloser to home, departing high pressure has kept the UP dry thus \nfar. This region of precipitation is slated to lift from west to east \nacross the UP this evening through Tuesday morning as the trough and \nsurface low work northward through northern Ontario. CAM ensembles \nlargely agree upon the southern half of the UP receiving a health \n0.25-0.75\" of rain, with the greatest probability for >0.5\" \nconcentrated in Menominee and Delta counties (60-90% chance). \nElsewhere, 0.25-0.50\" is expected. Apart from the rain, gusty winds \nare the other main concern of the short term forecast period. \nSoutherly wind gusts 25-35 mph will be common tonight with \npotentially stronger gusts near 40 mph in the downsloping areas of \nthe central UP. Tomorrow, the attendant surface low pressure system \ncold front will press through the region during the late morning to \nearly afternoon hours, veering winds to the west. Strong pressure \nrises upwards of 1-2 mb/hour and modest upper level cold air \nadvection will work to increase wind gusts on Lake Superior and in \nthe Keweenaw between 40-50 mph with occasional gusts to 55 mph \nshould mixing be able to tap into higher gusts aloft. A Windy \nAdvisory has been issued for N Houghton and Keweenaw Counties from \n15-00z Tuesday (10a-8p local). \n\nMoving into the rest of the week, expect generally quieter weather \nas zonal flow in the upper levels and shortwave ridges moving \nthrough the U.P. generally keep active weather away. While this \nshould keep temperatures fairly seasonable for the rest of the week, \nthis does also support dry weather continuing until the end of the \nforecast period, provided we don't get grazed by a nearby shortwave.",
